    T["B-tree indexes in databases"] = {
        "_cat": "technology",
        "what": "a self-balancing tree data structure used by most relational databases as the default index type, allowing logarithmic-time lookup, insertion, and range scans on sorted keys, optimized for storage on block devices like disks and SSDs",
        "how": "a B-tree (or its B+-tree variant) keeps keys in nodes that pack many keys per node (matching disk page size), keeps leaves at the same depth, and rebalances on insert and delete. Searches traverse from root to leaf, doing one page read per level. With branching factors in the hundreds, billion-row tables typically need only 4 or 5 page reads per lookup",
        "why": "B-trees turn O(N) full-table scans into O(log N) lookups for indexed queries, support range queries efficiently, and remain the workhorse index across PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle, and SQLite. Their performance shapes how database schemas are designed and queries optimized",
        "vs": "B-trees differ from hash indexes, which support equality lookups in O(1) average time but no range scans. They differ from LSM trees (used by Cassandra, RocksDB, LevelDB), which optimize writes via sequential log segments at the cost of slower reads. They differ from bitmap indexes used in analytical databases",
        "ex": "querying 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE last_name = ?' on a 100-million-row table takes microseconds with a B-tree index on last_name and full seconds without. Range scans like 'WHERE age BETWEEN 30 AND 40' also benefit because B-trees keep keys sorted",
        "mis": "people think indexes always speed up queries. Indexes slow down writes (every insert must update each index) and can hurt performance for queries that touch large fractions of rows, where a full scan beats random index lookups. Another myth is that indexes are free; they consume substantial storage and memory",
    }

    T["how SSDs work"] = {
        "_cat": "technology",
        "what": "solid-state drives are non-volatile storage devices that use NAND flash memory cells to store data without moving parts, offering much faster random access and lower latency than spinning hard disks at higher cost per byte",
        "how": "each NAND cell traps electrons in a floating gate to represent one or more bits (SLC, MLC, TLC, QLC encode 1, 2, 3, 4 bits per cell). The controller manages flash translation (mapping logical addresses to physical pages), wear leveling (spreading writes across cells), garbage collection (consolidating valid data so erase blocks can be reclaimed), and ECC (correcting bit errors that accumulate with wear)",
        "why": "SSDs transformed personal computing and the data center: laptops boot in seconds, databases handle far more transactions, and entire architectures (caching layers, in-memory databases) shifted as random I/O latency dropped from milliseconds to microseconds",
        "vs": "SSDs differ from HDDs by having no moving parts, much faster random access, lower latency, but historically higher cost per byte and finite write endurance. They differ from RAM by being non-volatile but slower (microseconds vs nanoseconds), and from hybrid drives by using only flash storage",
        "ex": "a modern NVMe SSD reads at over 5 gigabytes per second sequentially and handles hundreds of thousands of random IOPS, compared to a 7,200 RPM HDD's 100-200 IOPS. The difference reshapes how operating systems schedule I/O and how applications structure data layout",
        "mis": "people think SSDs wear out quickly. Modern consumer drives last hundreds of terabytes of writes, far more than typical lifetime usage. Another myth is that defragmentation helps SSDs; on flash, sequential and random access have similar performance, and defragmenting wastes write endurance",
    }

